{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"9d4c942e-ffbe-4d43-a2ba-097a8f16178d","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I have successfully built the `lab-12-components` lab for CS 6210 Advanced Operating Systems.\n\nHere's a summary of the actions taken:\n- **Concept Integration**: Read through the `L05e` notes to align the lab with the Ensemble system's component-based design cycle, focusing on NuPrl-driven Common Case Predicate (CCP) bypass paths.\n- **Python Implementation**: Created a layered micro-protocol stack emulator in `stack.py` with layers for fragmentation, ordering, checksumming, and 20 generic dummy layers simulating a real, deep network stack. I implemented a bypass optimization that uses CCPs (checking for in-order delivery, no fragmentation, and valid checksums) to entirely skip multi-layered overhead.\n- **Testing \u0026 Measurement**: Built a `Makefile` mapping to `all`, `run`, `test`, `clean`, and `fetch`. Tests simulate fast paths, slow paths, out-of-order packets, and payload fragmentation. Captures from `make run` in the VM environment display ~40% latency savings natively using the bypass path.\n- **Documentation**: Developed `README.md` containing performance findings, how the CCP works underneath, 3 experimentation prompts, and 3 theoretical questions with answers. The frontmatter remains properly intact. \n- **Delivery**: Created `.gitignore` and `expected-output.txt` alongside `stack.py`, `Makefile`, and `README.md`. Code was appropriately git committed with a conventional commit message. A formal report has been written out to `/Users/shreejitverma/github/career-ops/library/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/CS6210-AOS/crew/runner/reports/lab-12-components.md`.\n","duration_seconds":187.781973,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":135999,"output_tokens":15305,"thinking_tokens":6230,"cache_read_tokens":1122909,"total_tokens":151304}}}
